# Force LF in the working tree on every platform. The toolchain expects LF
# everywhere — rustfmt (`newline_style = "Unix"`), taplo, and `.editorconfig`.
# Without this, Git's `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) checks files out
# as CRLF, so formatting checks and Lefthook can fail on a native Windows host
# even though the repository blobs are LF.
# `text=auto` still lets Git auto-detect and leave binaries alone.
#
# Existing Windows clones: after pulling this file, run once to refresh the working
# tree to LF:  git add --renormalize . && git checkout .
* text=auto eol=lf

# Conformance fixtures encode exact code points and exact expected advances. They
# are compared byte-exact, so never rewrite LF <-> CRLF inside them.
**/tests/fixtures/** -text
fuzz/corpus/** -text

# The conformance suite is a published deliverable rather than a fixture: it is read
# by implementations that are not this one, and `conform --check` refuses a case file
# holding a CR. It is written with LF everywhere, on every platform.
crates/kumihan-conformance/protocol.schema.json text eol=lf
crates/kumihan-conformance/suite.ndjson text eol=lf

# The vendored upstream snapshot is verified byte for byte against the SHA-256 in
# spec/PROVENANCE.toml, so Git must never rewrite a line ending inside it. As
# retrieved, every one of these files is already LF throughout, so the marker
# records an invariant rather than protecting a difference — which is the point:
# an upstream revision that arrives with CRLF must change the digest and fail
# `attest --digests`, not be silently normalized into agreement with the old one.
spec/snapshot/** -text
spec/upstream/** -text
